ped that communicates  with the drone and gets positions from  the feet of the vikon to feedback to the  drone to move to positions that we pre  defined with the vikon camera system in  the fiducial marks and so what were able  to show once we did that is we were  actually able to do a successful pic in  place of a washer of picking up a washer  so you'll see the drone descend over to  one table and what it's doing there with  the electromagnet is picking up a  metallic washer flying to another  station and then dropping that off if  you watch this video  sort of successful and successful enough  that we figured we could do an SBIR to  actually see what the response would be  and there was actually tremendous I  think this was one of the SBIR is that  we issued an end cats that we receive  the most responses ever and ended up  meeting or we awarded three phase one  proposals and we wanted to actually see  if someone could meet this challenge  with this autonomous pickup and delivery  of an actual plate if you can actually  incorporate the low-level design and so  as an example one of the awardees was  actually able to come up with their own  custom canted rotor hex copter for all  the stability issues that you saw in  some of those videos is this provides  six degrees of freedom control in  translating XY plane on maintaining  level altitude and and and really what  that means in this case is there's no  picture at all when it moves and the  total weight of this drone is about 2.1  kilograms and it's actually has room to  shave maybe 400 to 500 grams from this  and so really what this turned into  doing as well is the gripper design and  integrating that with the control system  so it account for that center of mass  but if you remember back to our previous  slide with the old system is don't  reinvent the wheel is if we know the  stuff joint is very effective and very  and what it does mechanically is let's  go with what's known and create a  gripper which can be easily actuated to  actually pick up a plate and we know  it's rock solid and so will your  watching this video here is you'll  actually see this drone take off with  the same bike on camera systems and this  was on an mi t--'s lab working in  conjunction with career because they  were one of the SBIR award EES based  upon some of the MCAT specifications and  it's really been a fun project to see  here and what you'll see it is actually  picked this plate up take it back and  then put the plate back in and so if you  want to show up watch this video  you  you  you  and so what you saw there was a the  slight adjustment with the control  system for the center of gravity is when  I pick up the plate he saw pinched  slightly down and I'm gonna put the  plate home you saw it sort of raised its  arm and victory that I did it but what  it shows is with technology currently  available today is it's feasible that  you can use an autonomous vehicle to  move plates from point A to point B and  we know that this is actually very  preliminary and there's challenges such  as rotor wash but it introduces the  concept of autonomy of systems  communicating one another that were  never intended to which is really  exciting and just one last example is  what would this look like potentially if  we had multiple drones and a shared  environment this is showing dynamic  collision avoidance but really for a  hiker but screaming challenge we would  have fixed stations that these drones  would fly to but this is showing  conceptually rerouting so what you're  going to see here is the squares on the  ground and there's red green and blue or  where the drone is supposed to go the  circle is where the drone is suppose is  actually currently located and what  you'll see is the intersect as the  drones can dynamically avoid colliding  with one another as the circle meets up  at the square if you want to show this  video  don't be a demon Rosa  [Music]  and so what you're able to see from this  is not only can one drone actually do a  pick in place you can have several  drones working in tandem and actually  avoid collision there or technically  people and remember this is any  autonomous vehicle it could be a  land-based instrument is actually more  suitable towards lab environment as  opposed to a drone but it's really  possible and so really what we want to  show in this presentation is as you're  expanding your automation and you're  looking from where you currently are to  where you want to be is if you look at  devices from the component level and  then how that component impacts the  device and then how that device impacts  the overall system and then how that  distributed systems can actually work  together and then the possibility of  integrating these distributed systems is  there you can actually have the  potential for autonomous assays and so  if you imagine from this perspective if  we have our high throughput screening  system is one distributed system with  all peripheral devices and then we have  our selectee for example that's  generating cells is if it was capable of  sending event which is it which it is  when a plate is available and we can  integrate that with an overall  controlling architecture is that could  actually initiate a new assay on a  completely independent system if we have  some play transportation mechanism and  that's really geared towards what we're  shooting for eventually is this concept  of autonomous so we look at the  trajectory of automation over the years  it was a long time ago now that things  were computerized and then it went  through this step and where they're  actually automated and we're looking for  now from the both be sustainable  distributed but really the goal is once  again autonomous and we're not there yet  and there's plenty of work to do but  we're showing through some of the work  we're doing in n cats and with many more  collaborators and both partnerships and  SBIR contracts is we're really 