er the capability once again to be  able to monitor and log every event  because it's hard to say when you know  an event will become important and you  might have an experimental design down  the road that you might want to track  something and if you don't have the  capability up front  it could really limit you so one of the  first step is make sure it has  monitoring and monitoring control  capabilities but even furthermore make  sure it has the ability to have  programmatic control meaning you can get  the scan  to do something programmatically from an  external application  many of the schedulers available both  commercial and open source these days  have that capability be it direct or  even there's you can do plenty of things  to gain scheduling software but a real  consideration especially when I shoot  towards more advanced automation and  more tricky experiments is having a very  fine level of programmatic control of  the scheduling software what direction  do you see going forward for  high-throughput screening systems I  think what we're seeing now is the days  of the monolithic larger system even the  ones that we have back to the early  2000s they're really they're still in  use and at certain in certain places but  I think they're going away  we're towards a more modular approach we  can actually split up your entire  process that maybe takes place on  several systems not just one so I think  it's a mixture of having this this  modular approach and also this this this  nimble approach so it can be  reconfigured you can still in the  libraries are still larger and larger  size but in many cases more focused  specific libraries but there's more  complex when you coming with assays and  so you want to make sure that if you  design a system you can keep up with  that actual additional complexity and  that's something that we're seeing again  cats and also some of our other folks  involved in this space are sharing some  of that those thoughts as well can